  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">From the beginning of the COVID-19 pandemic in Kuwait, the International Domestic Workers Federation (IDWF), through its affiliates, provided support to migrant workers, starting with a rapid assessment of the specific vulnerability and needs of migrant communities in Kuwait (an online survey, reaching more than 3,000 migrant workers). 
IDWF also supported virtual meetings with migrant worker community representatives to ensure that food distribution (from which more than 3,500 people had already benefited as at May 2020) could reach the most vulnerable community members, and to facilitate their needs being heard by the Kuwait government, embassies of countries of origin (including the Philippines embassy), local CSOs, philanthropic organizations and other stakeholders. 
IDWF supported migrant worker representatives to afford assistance in around 1,000 labour disputes (including domestic workers) by providing information, advice or referrals, and facilitated 20 online migrant forums, each attended by 15-25 workers to share information on workers&apos; rights. With support from the Government of Switzerland, the ILO provided technical support to develop the questionnaire for the online rapid assessment (March-April 2020) and domestic worker representatives were trained according to a training programme developed by the IDWF. The ILO provided financial support for facilitating bi-weekly virtual meetings between community leaders, including the African community, in order to identify urgent humanitarian and labour-related priorities and methods of support (April &#x2013; August 2020). In 2021, the ILO continued its support to strengthening the capacities of domestic worker representatives in Kuwait including through a structured training programme and supporting solidarity, including through the fourth annual International Domestic Workers&apos; Day celebration (virtual), which included more than 60 participants, many of them domestic workers from the Philippines, Ethiopia and other African countries such as Sierra Leone and Burkina Faso.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">7.5.2. Number of countries with new or improved services to protect the labour rights of migrant workers</narrative>
